<chapter xml:id="chapter-6" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Mass Conservation</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
        The three scalar component equations of motion presented in <xref first="eqn-motion-x" last="eqn-motion-z" text="type-global" />
        of Chapter 5 have five unknown dependent variables<mdash /><m>u</m>, <m>v</m>, <m>w</m>, <m>p</m>, and <m>\rho</m><mdash />that are
        functions of the four independent variables <m>x</m>, <m>y</m>, <m>z</m>, and <m>t</m>.
        Since there are two more unknowns than there are equations,
        and we would like to uniquely solve for each unknown to describe the state and evolution of the atmosphere,
        we must produce two further independent equations in the unknowns.
    </p>

    <p>
        In Chapter 6,
        we will consider mass to obtain an additional necessary equation.
        We further will consider energy in Chapter 7 to close the system.
        When we have closed the system (by having as many equations as unknowns),
        we have fully captured the atmospheric dynamics described by the system.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <xi:include href="ch6-section-lagrangian.ptx" />

</chapter>